00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
which leads us into another basketball  story basketball star Kevin Love is  drawing a new attention to mental health  a problem for many Americans practicing  social distancing of course because of  the corona virus of pandemic one in five  American adults experiences some form of  mental illness each year the five-time  NBA all-star has a history of anxiety  depression and panic attacks and he's  become a spokesman on the issue he joins  us from his home in Cleveland Kevin Love  good morning thanks so much for being  with us thanks for having me I  appreciate it you donated $100,000 to  arena workers and several other players  have followed suit why was it important  for you to make that gesture just  understanding that this is gonna be such  a tough time to navigate it was actually  the Thursday morning after Adam silver  our Commissioner had postponed the  season and I was just thinking about you  know how these people are so much a part  of my story our story as NBA players we  see them at least 41 games home games  out of the year as well as other events  and these are these are people that you  start to develop a first-name basis with  the first-name relationship with people  that you know I've been in Cleveland now  six years who I see every time coming  into the game I'm giving them a bump  I'm asking them how their family is so  just knowing that this is such a tough  time than that navigate it's gonna be  such a stressful time anxiety ridden and  just the fear of unknown is gonna be  there so much I was just thinking about  all that stuff and thought it'd be great  to take care of the people who have been  taking care of me and supporting me and  and the Cavs organization for so long so  you know decided to you know donate to  them the arena workers some most of them  I feel like are living paycheck to  paycheck not knowing when food's gonna  be put on the table if they're going to  be able to take care of their families  so I thought it was super very important  to take care of them and then you saw  the Cavs stepped up and  you know said that they would pay the  organization said that they would pay  for all the games you know that that  aren't going to go on a lot of the  events that aren't going to go on and  then you saw different players stepping  up and and and doing the same thing or  donating meals you know committing money  to the nurses and the doctors and  several different hospitals so it just  kind of took on a life of its own and I  was incredibly happy to see other  players step up and do something similar  it was it was really cool to see that I  think so - Kevin first let's say Bravo  to you number one that you know the  workers names a lot of players don't but  you do  and the fact that they saw what you did  and they followed your lead honestly I  think it says something about who you  are as a person as a man and how you  live your life so Bravo Bravo to you on  that you've also been so outspoken about  your own I mean that you've been so  outspoken about your own mental health  issues and your own anxieties how have  you been coping during this time for me  just stay in active and making sure that  I have a routine so much of the time  when that has taken away from me I think  just by nature being a basketball player  I'm so used to having that routine so  making sure I'm getting up early I'm  getting the proper nutrition I'm taking  care of my body so physically taking  care of my body and then just  understanding what my core competencies  are and my my pillars in my life like  always working on my relationships  checking in with with friends and family  making sure that I'm you know while I'm  distancing social distancing I'm not  isolating myself I'm reaching out to my  family friends doing constant  communication because the device is here  you know so even if it's virtually just  staying in touch with everybody I'm  making sure that I'm doing that but just  continuing to move thankfully I have a  dog that forces me to get outside so  yeah things like that yeah that  certainly helps Kevin you know I tell  you what you'd have to be made of stone  to not be feeling a little bit of  anxiety a little bit of sadness right  now so what's your messy  to people who may be struggling with  this cataclysmic event we're all facing  yeah I think it's like I said earlier an  incredibly tough time to to navigate and  I would say my message would just be  it's it's it's normal to feel this way  the only way we're gonna get through it  is together I've said you know since  this you know started happening and we  were having to you know isolate  ourselves and quarantine that it's  normal to feel this way and the only way  we're gonna get out of this is together  like you know human beings are so  resilient and we will get through this  and I think you know the worst thing  that can happen is if we come out of  this unchanged we have to be able to  change and be there for each other and  be better so I do think there will be a  lot of great things that that come out  of this we may not be able to see it  right now and so much is changing every  single day and we don't know what life  is going to look like after this let  alone the mental health of a lot of  people coming out of this but nothing  nothing unites us like the common and  the same enemy so I would say just can  we continue to commit to that sense of  community and know that everybody's  going through something at this point  and at this time so just behind and and  take care of the next man and take care  of the next woman Kevin Love mr. Kevin  Love amen to that thank you very much  and that is one cool piece of barnwood  behind you thank you for being here

